AB  - Summary
Graphene supported on a substrate in contact with water underpins a wide range of processes and technologies, yet its wettability remains controversial. Understanding how substrate charges and graphene’s properties influence water organization is crucial. Here, we combine heterodyne-detected sum-frequency generation (HD-SFG) spectroscopy with molecular dynamics simulations to investigate CaF2-supported graphene interfaces in contact with water. We find that the interfacial water orientation is primarily governed by the CaF2 substrate’s pH-dependent local electrostatics, confirming graphene’s macroscopic wetting transparency. However, at the nanoscale, graphene’s polarizability gives rise to a local reorientation of interfacial water above substrate charges, partially counteracting the electrostatic field originating from the substrate and revealing a subtle structural ordering that is masked in spatially averaged measurements. These insights elucidate the molecular origins of graphene’s wetting behavior and suggest new avenues for tailoring interfacial phenomena in graphene-based nanofluidic, sensing, and energy applications.
