AB  - Due to the existence of hotspots, revealing the lateral heat transfer mechanism of key materials in GaN power chips is crucial for the development of high-performance chips. In this study, influencing mechanism of nano-grooves formed on a SiC substrate on the thermal conductivity of graphene layer is investigated by molecular dynamics simulation. The results show that the thermal conductivities of graphene on the SiC surface with nano-grooves are reduced between 16.86% and 26.41% as compared with the model without nano-groove. Analysis reveals that the nano-grooves cause a blue shift of the phonon density of state in graphene in the low frequency region of 0–24 THz and a shift of phonon modes to localized modes in the 20–24 THz, which are not conducive to heat conduction. In addition, stress concentration occurs at the nano-grooves for all the graphene, and the thermal barrier effect leads to the decrease of the thermal conductivities of graphene. Interestingly, the variation of the nano-groove sizes has little effect on the thermal conductivities of graphene, which can be explained by the consistency of the fluctuation degree between the participation ratio of phonons in the 20–24 THz, the stress values of graphene at the nano-grooves and the thermal conductivities of graphene. These findings provide important theoretical guidance and practical reference value for the thermal design and thermal management of GaN power chips.
