AB  - Marine biofouling has been a major technical and economic challenge for offshore steel structures. This study reports the development of a graphene nanoplatelets (GNP)-modified cementitious coating (GCC) for broad-spectrum antifouling protection. Composite coatings incorporating varying graphene dosages (0–1.00 wt%) were fabricated and brush-applied onto S355 steel substrates. The bioreceptivity and antifouling performance of the coatings were systematically evaluated under both laboratory conditions and real marine exposure, targeting bacterial attachment as well as macrofouling settlement by tubeworm/polychaete-like and juvenile barnacle-/limpet-like organisms. Coating characterisation included pore structures, surface roughness, water contact angle, interfacial bonding, microstructural features, and fouling coverage. The results demonstrated that graphene incorporation effectively suppressed bacterial attachment over 48 h and macrofouling colonisation during an eight-week marine exposure. An optimal antifouling performance was observed at a graphene dosage of 0.30 wt%, reducing the total bacterial surface density by approximately two orders of magnitude compared with the uncoated reference, while also exhibiting the highest interfacial adhesion among all coatings. Compared with a commercial cuprous oxide-based antifouling coating, the developed GCC also showed better durability under marine exposure. The enhanced antifouling behaviour is attributed to a combination of reduced bioreceptivity arising from graphene-induced microstructural densification and surface homogenisation, together with graphene-enabled interfacial effects that collectively impair microbial attachment and viability. Overall, the proposed coating strategy offers a promising and sustainable approach for inhibiting fouling and extending the service life of offshore steel structures.
