AB  - This paper presents the results of an experimental research to investigate the effects of a dual Impressed Current Cathodic Protection–Structural Strengthening (ICCP–SS) system to control steel corrosion and store load carrying capacity of reinforced concrete (RC) beams susceptible to chloride-induced steel corrosion, employing fibre-reinforced cementitious matrix (FRCM) composite as both the cathode and the strengthening material. The finding of this project will provide the basis for a practical and affordable solution to significantly enhance the service life of FRCM composite used in ICCP-SS intervention method, thus improving durability of strengthened RC structures. Four-point bending tests were conducted on 14 corroded RC beams repaired by ICCP–SS, considering three graphene dosages (0%, 0.035%, and 0.075% by cement weight) and three ICCP current densities (20, 50, and 100 mA/m²). All beam specimens underwent approximately 700 days of accelerated corrosion, and 650, 590, or 450 days of ICCP depending on the current density. Results confirm graphene’s benefits under extended ICCP exposure: while beams without graphene but subjected to high ICCP current densities (100 mA/m²) resulted in a small increase of 6.3% comparing to corroded specimens without any treatment, beams with graphene (0.035% and 0.07% by weight of cement) yielded more significant capacity increase by 13.4% and 15%, respectively. Experimental-based service-life predictions indicate that graphene extends the service life of the repaired beams by up to 55.8% (∼20 years extension) under an ICCP current of 20 mA/m², assuming that structural deterioration is solely related to the total amount of electrons applied.
