AB  - T-graphene, an allotrope of the graphene family, has attracted a lot of attention for its potential use in technological applications, broadening the use of the family of carbon-based materials. In this work, we study the effect of the Rashba spin–orbit coupling on the strained T-graphene monolayer utilizing the tight-binding model. The energy band-structure, density of states, electronic heat capacity and magnetic susceptibility have been studied under the above conditions. Strained T-graphene under Rashba coupling continues to maintain the metallic characteristics. Moreover, strain modifies the band-structure leading to changes in the Fermi energy and high symmetry points of the lattice. Heat-capacity and magnetic susceptibility increase with increasing Rashba coupling. The above study provides a platform for exploring the structure–property relationship in low-dimensional materials under external forces, which might be relevant for future experimental and theoretical investigations of T-graphene-based devices.
