AB  - Copper (Cu)–Graphene composites were fabricated by powder metallurgy with graphene contents of 0–15 wt% and belt-sintered at 870 °C for 2 h under an N₂–H₂ atmosphere to quantify the influence of high graphene loading on microstructure and heat transport. The measured density decreased from 7.183 to 5.076 g/cm³ (relative density: 80.17–90.64%) as the graphene content increased from 0 to 15 wt%. Hardness declined from 49.3 ± 1.53 HRBW to 36–39 HRBW at 15 wt% graphene. Quantitative EDS-based dispersion analysis demonstrated increasing heterogeneity beyond 5 wt% graphene (σ = 2.85 at 5 wt%, 5.20 at 10 wt%, and 5.59 at 15 wt%). XRD confirmed retention of the FCC Cu phase without oxide formation, and Raman analysis revealed pronounced structural disorder at 15 wt% graphene (ID/IG = 2.718). At 30 °C, the thermal conductivity values were 117.97 W/m·K (5 wt% graphene), 40.79 W/m·K (10 wt% graphene), and 80.42 W/m·K (15 wt% graphene) respectively. Despite the increase in relative density, thermal conductivity decreased beyond 5 wt% graphene, indicating that dispersion instability and Cu–graphene interfacial resistance govern heat transport. These findings establish a graphene content threshold beyond which interfacial resistance and porosity dominate thermal transport, thereby providing quantitative design guidance for Cu–based MEMS thermal management systems.
